          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "![](path-62a0f648e2768df5001f.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(2,-2\\right)$ to $B=\\left(4,-1\\right)$, as shown.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 t + 2,t - 2\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $x'(t)=2$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=6 t\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(6 t\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}12 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}12 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[6 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}x$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used.",
