          ":question/id": "0691579b-c5a0-581d-b893-afb9ccfaaa52",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "![](path-bbdac4701b5dc7d6eae7.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- 2 y e^{1 - \\frac{2 x^{2}}{9}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ follows the indicated arc of the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4}=1$ counterclockwise from $A=\\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},1\\right)$ to $B=\\left(0,2\\right)$, as shown.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ at $A$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=- 4 e^{1 - 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 4 e^{1 - 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}- 8 e^{1 - 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $\\sin^2 t+\\cos^2 t=1$ and set $u=\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The exponent becomes $1 - \\frac{2 x^{2}}{9}=2 u - 1$. The new bounds are $u=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $u=1$, respectively.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{4}}^{1}- 4 e^{2 u - 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 e^{2 u - 1}\\right]_{\\frac{1}{4}}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(- 2 e\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 e + \\frac{2}{e^{\\frac{1}{2}}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used.",
