          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/problem": "![](path-c8ca93c8dafba94810a4.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi y \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(1,2\\right)$ to $B=\\left(1,0\\right)$, as shown.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1,2 - 2 t\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=-2$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(2 - 2 t\\right) \\right)} + 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(2 - 2 t\\right) \\right)} + 1\\right)\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 4 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(2 - 2 t\\right) \\right)} - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor the trigonometric term, the argument derivative is $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\pi \\left(2 - 2 t\\right)\\right)=- 2 \\pi$. Keep that argument when applying the sine or cosine primitive.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}- 4 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi \\left(2 - 2 t\\right) \\right)} - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 t - 2 \\cos{\\left(\\pi \\left(2 - 2 t\\right) \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-4\\right)-\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\n&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used.",
